The Security Council,

Recalling its resolutions 2118 (2013), 2209 (2015), 2235 (2015), 2314 (2016), and 2319 (2016),

Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, territorial integrity and national unity of the Syrian Arab Republic, and that the only sustainable solution to the current crisis in the Syrian Arab Republic is through an inclusive and Syrian-led political process,

Determined to hold accountable those responsible for the use of chemical weapons, and reaffirming that the use of chemical weapons constitutes a serious violation of international law and a threat to international peace and security,

Expressing grave concern at the findings of the reports of the Organisation for the Prohibition of Chemical Weapons-United Nations Joint Investigative Mechanism (JIM) (S/2016/738/Rev.1 and S/2016/888), which concluded that the Syrian Arab Armed Forces were involved in the use of toxic chemicals as weapons in Talmenes on 21 April 2014, in Sarmin on 16 March 2015, and in Qmenas on 16 March 2015, and that the Islamic State in Iraq and the Levant (ISIL, also known as Da'esh) was involved in the use of sulfur mustard in Marea on 21 August 2015,

Condemning in the strongest terms any use of any toxic chemical as a weapon, by any party in the Syrian Arab Republic, and reiterating that individuals, entities, groups, or governments responsible for any use of chemical weapons must be held accountable,

Recalling the obligations of the Syrian Arab Republic under the Convention on the Prohibition of the Development, Production, Stockpiling and Use of Chemical Weapons and on their Destruction (Chemical Weapons Convention) and resolution 2118 (2013),

Acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations,

1. Condemns in the strongest terms the use of toxic chemicals as weapons in the Syrian Arab Republic, in particular the attacks investigated by the OPCW-UN Joint Investigative Mechanism;

2. Demands that all parties in the Syrian Arab Republic immediately cease the use of toxic chemicals as weapons;

3. Decides that all Member States shall take the necessary measures to prevent the direct or indirect supply, sale, or transfer to the Syrian Arab Republic, by their nationals or from their territories, or using their flag vessels or aircraft, whether or not originating in their territories, of chlorine or other toxic chemicals, or dual-use equipment and materials that could be used to manufacture chemical weapons, as well as helicopters and related materiel, including spare parts;

4. Decides that all Member States shall take the necessary measures to prevent the entry into or transit through their territories of the individuals listed in Annex I to this resolution, provided that nothing in this paragraph shall oblige a State to refuse its own nationals entry into its territory;

5. Decides that all Member States shall freeze without delay all funds, other financial assets and economic resources which are on their territories, which are owned or controlled, directly or indirectly, by the individuals or entities listed in Annex I and Annex II to this resolution, or by persons or entities acting on their behalf or at their direction;

6. Decides to establish, in accordance with rule 28 of its provisional rules of procedure, a Committee of the Security Council consisting of all the members of the Council, to undertake tasks including monitoring the implementation of the measures imposed in paragraphs 3, 4, and 5 of this resolution, designating additional individuals and entities, and considering requests for exemptions;

7. Requests the Secretary-General to establish for an initial period of eleven months, in consultation with the Committee, a group of up to six experts (Panel of Experts) under the direction of the Committee, to assist the Committee in carrying out its mandate;

8. Strongly urges all States to fully cooperate with the Committee and the Panel of Experts, as well as with the OPCW and the OPCW-UN Joint Investigative Mechanism;

9. Reiterates its determination to impose further measures under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations in the event of non-compliance with this resolution or resolution 2118 (2013);

10. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

